README.md
A Workflow engine on the Claude Agent SDK
A self-contained, ~180-line reimplementation of the Workflow orchestration engine that ships inside Claude Code — rebuilt on top of the public Claude Agent SDK (@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk) so you can use the same pattern in your own tool / TUI.

What "a workflow" is
A workflow is a plain-JS script that acts as a deterministic coordinator. It doesn't do the work itself — it spawns subagents, decides what runs concurrently vs sequentially, and threads each subagent's return value into the next step. The script gets six globals injected:

global	what it does
agent(prompt, opts?)	spawn ONE subagent → returns its result (string, or a validated object if opts.schema). The unit of work.
parallel(thunks)	barrier fan-out — run all concurrently, await all. Failed agents resolve to null.
pipeline(items, ...stages)	no-barrier per-item chain — item A can be in stage 3 while B is still in stage 1.
phase(title)	label the following agents (for progress UI).
log(msg)	emit a progress line.
budget	{ total, spent(), remaining() } — output-token accounting for loop guards.
args	whatever you passed into runWorkflow({ args }).
This is the same API surface as Claude Code's Workflow tool — see the mapping below.

How it maps to the Agent SDK
The whole engine is "one agent() = one query()" plus a management shell around it:

agent(prompt, {schema, model, tools})   →   query({ prompt, options })
                                              drain the async generator to the
                                              message.type === "result", then return
                                              result.structured_output (if schema)  or  result (text)
parallel([...])                          →   Promise.all under a counting semaphore
pipeline(items, s1, s2)                  →   per-item: s2(await s1(item)), all concurrent
concurrency cap                          →   min(16, cores - 2)        (same as Claude Code)
1000-agent lifetime cap                  →   runaway-loop backstop
budget / budget.remaining()              →   summed result.usage.output_tokens
schema: {...}                            →   options.outputFormat = { type:'json_schema', schema }
Each query() is its own isolated session with its own context — exactly what a Workflow subagent is. The subagent gets the SDK's built-in tools (Bash, Read, Write, Grep, WebSearch, …), so the engine never hand-writes a tool loop; it only orchestrates.

Two notes vs. the in-binary Claude Code engine:

Structured output uses the SDK's native outputFormat (Agent SDK v0.3.x+) and reads result.structured_output — no prompt-instructed JSON, no client-side validation needed.
Resume/journal and git-worktree isolation are omitted here for brevity (they're the same idea: memoize agent() by (prompt, opts); run file-writing agents in a worktree).
Install & run
npm i @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk      # v0.3.x+
# Auth: uses your existing Claude Code login (run `claude` once). No ANTHROPIC_API_KEY needed.

node run.mjs example.workflow.js                       # the bundled code-review example
node run.mjs example.workflow.js --budget 200000 --concurrency 8
run.mjs loads a .workflow.js file, runs it, and prints live progress. The example fans out one reviewer per dimension (bugs / security / perf), each returning schema-validated findings, then a synthesizer writes the report.

Writing a workflow
A workflow file is just a script body — no imports; the globals are injected. It can return a final value, which runWorkflow hands back as result.

phase('Find');
const SCHEMA = { type:'object', additionalProperties:false,
  required:['bugs'], properties:{ bugs:{ type:'array', items:{ type:'string' } } } };

// fan out N finders, each with shell+read tools, each forced to return JSON
const found = await parallel([1,2,3].map((i) => () =>
  agent(`Pass ${i}: run \`git diff\` and list any bugs.`,
        { label:`find:${i}`, tools:['Bash','Read','Grep'], schema:SCHEMA, model:'sonnet' })));

const bugs = found.filter(Boolean).flatMap((r) => r.bugs);
log(`${bugs.length} candidate bugs`);

phase('Verify');
// pipeline: each bug is verified independently, no barrier between find and verify
const verdicts = await pipeline(bugs,
  (bug) => agent(`Is this a real bug? "${bug}". Answer yes/no + why.`, { label:'verify', model:'haiku' }));

return verdicts;
agent() options
option	meaning
schema	JSON Schema → forces structured output; agent() returns the validated object (or null on failure).
model	'opus' / 'sonnet' / 'haiku' alias, or a full model id. Omit to inherit the SDK default.
tools	array of allowed tool names (['Bash','Read','Grep',…]). Omit for a no-tool text transform.
mcpServers	MCP server config passed straight through to the SDK.
maxTurns	agentic-turn cap (default 40 with tools, 6 without).
label / phase	progress-UI labels.
Wiring into a TUI
runWorkflow takes an onEvent callback — that's your render seam. Every lifecycle event flows through it; map them to your Ink/blessed/whatever view:

import { runWorkflow } from './workflow.mjs';

const rows = new Map();
const out = await runWorkflow(scriptSource, {
  args: { task: 'review the branch' },
  tokenBudget: 500_000,
  onEvent: (e) => {
    switch (e.type) {
      case 'phase':       header(e.title); break;
      case 'agent:start': rows.set(e.n, { label: e.label, state: 'running' }); render(rows); break;
      case 'agent:done':  rows.set(e.n, { label: e.label, state: 'done', ms: e.ms }); render(rows); break;
      case 'agent:error': rows.set(e.n, { label: e.label, state: 'error', err: e.error }); render(rows); break;
      case 'log':         footer(e.message); break;
    }
  },
});
// out = { result, agents, spentTokens, costUsd, logs }
Because the engine is just a function returning a promise, you can run it from a command handler, await it, and stream onEvent straight into your component state. result is whatever the workflow script returned (the synthesized report, the array of verdicts, etc.).

Files
workflow.mjs — the engine (runWorkflow).
example.workflow.js — a runnable review-and-synthesize workflow.
run.mjs — CLI runner + reference onEvent progress printer.
Caveats
permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions' runs subagents headless — they execute tools without prompting. Scope tools to what each agent actually needs.
The script runs in a node:vm sandbox with only the injected globals visible. It is not a security boundary against hostile scripts — it's there to give the script a clean, fixed API.
Cost is real: each agent() is a billed model call. Use tokenBudget + budget.remaining() to bound loops, and prefer haiku/sonnet for bulk fan-out, opus for synthesis.
example.workflow.js
// example.workflow.js — a runnable workflow script for workflow.mjs.
//
// Pattern: "review changed files across dimensions, then synthesize" — the canonical
// fan-out → structured-output → synthesize shape. Run with run.mjs (see README).
//
// The globals (agent, parallel, pipeline, phase, log, budget, args) are injected by the
// engine — you do NOT import anything here. The script can `return` a final value.

const FINDINGS_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: false,
  required: ['dimension', 'findings'],
  properties: {
    dimension: { type: 'string' },
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        additionalProperties: false,
        required: ['title', 'severity', 'detail'],
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['low', 'medium', 'high'] },
          detail: { type: 'string' },
        },
      },
    },
  },
};

const DIMENSIONS = [
  { key: 'bugs', prompt: 'Run `git diff` and review the changed code for correctness bugs.' },
  { key: 'security', prompt: 'Run `git diff` and review the changed code for security issues.' },
  { key: 'perf', prompt: 'Run `git diff` and review the changed code for performance problems.' },
];

phase('Review');

// One reviewer per dimension, all concurrent (capped by the semaphore). Each gets read+shell
// tools and is forced to return JSON matching FINDINGS_SCHEMA.
const reviews = await parallel(
  DIMENSIONS.map((d) => () =>
    agent(`${d.prompt}\nReturn your findings for the "${d.key}" dimension.`, {
      label: `review:${d.key}`,
      phase: 'Review',
      tools: ['Bash', 'Read', 'Grep'],
      schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA,
      model: 'sonnet',
    }),
  ),
);

const all = reviews.filter(Boolean).flatMap((r) => r.findings.map((f) => ({ ...f, dimension: r.dimension })));
log(`collected ${all.length} findings across ${reviews.filter(Boolean).length} dimensions`);

if (all.length === 0) return { summary: 'No findings.', findings: [] };

phase('Synthesize');

// One synthesizer reads all findings and writes the report. No schema → returns raw markdown.
const report = await agent(
  `These are code-review findings (JSON):\n${JSON.stringify(all, null, 2)}\n\n` +
    'Write a concise markdown review: group by severity, dedupe overlaps, lead with the ' +
    'highest-impact issues. Output ONLY the markdown.',
  { label: 'synthesize', phase: 'Synthesize', model: 'opus' },
);

return { summary: report, findings: all };
run.mjs
#!/usr/bin/env node
// run.mjs — load a .workflow.js file and run it, printing live progress.
//
//   node run.mjs example.workflow.js
//   node run.mjs example.workflow.js --budget 200000 --concurrency 8
//
// The onEvent callback below is the seam you replace with your TUI renderer (Ink/blessed/etc.):
// every agent:start / agent:done / phase / log event flows through it.

import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { runWorkflow } from './workflow.mjs';

const [, , file, ...rest] = process.argv;
if (!file) {
  console.error('usage: node run.mjs <file.workflow.js> [--budget N] [--concurrency N]');
  process.exit(1);
}
const flag = (name, d) => {
  const i = rest.indexOf(name);
  return i >= 0 ? Number(rest[i + 1]) : d;
};

const src = await readFile(file, 'utf8');

// ── TUI seam: render however you like. Here we just log to stderr. ────────────────────────
const onEvent = (e) => {
  if (e.type === 'phase') console.error(`\n▸ ${e.title}`);
  else if (e.type === 'agent:start') console.error(`  ⟳ [${e.n}] ${e.label}`);
  else if (e.type === 'agent:done') console.error(`  ✓ [${e.n}] ${e.label} (${e.ms}ms)`);
  else if (e.type === 'agent:error') console.error(`  ✗ [${e.n}] ${e.label} — ${e.error}`);
  else if (e.type === 'log') console.error(`  · ${e.message}`);
};

const out = await runWorkflow(src, {
  cwd: process.cwd(),
  tokenBudget: flag('--budget', null),
  concurrency: flag('--concurrency', undefined),
  onEvent,
});

console.error(`\n— done: ${out.agents} agents, ${out.spentTokens} output tokens, $${out.costUsd.toFixed(4)} —\n`);
console.log(typeof out.result === 'string' ? out.result : JSON.stringify(out.result, null, 2));
